Here is a Python code that can help with this task.

```python
import re

# Read the C# file
with open('your_file.cs', 'r') as file:
    data = file.read()

# Find all comments starting with '@@@'
matches = re.findall(r'\/\/\/\s@\@\@\s(.*?)$', data, re.MULTILINE)

# Extract the part up to the first ';' after each comment
parsed_matches = [re.match(r'(.*?);', match).group(1) for match in matches]

# Output the found asserts
for match in parsed_matches:
    # Replace the variable names with more generic names
    # As we can't run Pex on a real program with uninstantiated properties and fields,
    #  and C# does not support such thing in assert statement.
    # But for this purpose we can safely replace them with dummy ones.
    match = re.sub(r'Old_arrListCount|Old_x|Old_index|Old_arrListIndexOfX|Old_arrListLastIndexOfX|Old_arrListContainsX|New_Ret|New_arrListCount|New_arrListIndexOfX|New_arrListLastIndexOfX|New_arrListContainsX|New_containsNewRet', 'genericName', match)
    print(f"Debug.Assert({match});")
```

This code reads the C# file line by line and uses regex to find all comments starting with '@@@'. It then matches the part of the comment string up to the first semicolon (after removing the 'Debug.Assert' prefix).

Please, Note: This code assumes that all the code after "@@@" has the pattern "condition;", if not, then this regex won't work.
Also, I assumed that we're not really interested in the properties before applying Pex, so they were replaced with generic names. For a real program with uninstantiated properties and fields, you would need to replace the property names with actual values.

Also, please consider that you will need to tweak the regex to fit your actual comments' style. Comments in C# could be single '//' or multiline '/* ... */' and this script assumes the single-line style.--------------------```cs
